\section{Analysis}\label{sec:analysis}

The five-way comparison (\S\ref{sec:experiments}) yields a paradox: training succeeds but inference fails, and no single architectural factor explains the gap. This section resolves it through a causal exclusion chain; each subsection kills one alternative hypothesis: \S\ref{subsec:gate-conditioning} kills ``turn conditioning rescues late-turn attention''; \S\ref{subsec:pcgrad} kills ``gradient conflict prevents GAR convergence''; \S\ref{subsec:dist-shift} identifies exposure bias as the surviving explanation. \S\ref{subsec:mechanism-specificity} confirms selectivity via an orthogonal metric, and \S\ref{subsec:cross-model} confirms generalization across architectures.

\subsection{Gate Turn Conditioning}\label{subsec:gate-conditioning}

We compare gate values at turn~1 (early) and turn~21 (late) across all 28 layers. The gates invert the design intent: in 27/28 layers, gate values are \emph{lower} at turn~1 than turn~21 (Wilcoxon $p = 3.73 \times 10^{-8}$, $r = 0.975$); e.g., layer~23 shifts from 0.769 to 0.979. NTP pressure pushes gates toward 1.0, a convergence pattern reminiscent of attention collapse in deep transformers \citep{dong2021attention}, yielding inference-time values of 0.93--0.98 (2--7\% attenuation; Table~\ref{tab:four-way}; full gate profiles in Appendix~\ref{sec:appendix-gate-profile}). The Uniform Gate learns comparable per-layer variation (L3\,=\,0.777, L27\,=\,1.000) despite lacking turn conditioning, confirming the structure arises from LoRA$+$GAR pressure (Figure~\ref{fig:gate-shift}a). Does the NTP-GAR gradient conflict prevent the intended behavior?

\subsection{PCGrad Gradient Conflict Exclusion}\label{subsec:pcgrad}

NTP and GAR gradients oppose in 68--79\% of layers (cosine similarity $-$0.089$\to$$-$0.042 across epochs), with NTP magnitude dominance growing from 7.5$\times$ to 14.7$\times$ (full gradient statistics in Appendix~\ref{sec:appendix-gradient}). If this conflict prevents GAR convergence, eliminating it via PCGrad \citep{yu2020pcgrad}, which projects conflicting gradients onto the other's normal plane, should prevent the collapse. To our knowledge, this is the first application of PCGrad as a \emph{diagnostic} tool rather than an optimization method.

PCGrad shifts cosine similarity to oscillate around zero, yet TF metrics are unchanged. AR GAR \emph{worsens}: 0.064$\to$0.030 ($p$\,=\,0.002; Figure~\ref{fig:training-pcgrad}b in Appendix~\ref{sec:appendix-gradient}). By \emph{modus tollens}: removing gradient conflict does not prevent collapse; therefore it is not the cause. The 7.5--14.7$\times$ magnitude dominance renders gradient conflict inconsequential. Cross-model analysis confirms this pattern: Llama-3.1-8B gradients are near-orthogonal (cos\,=\,+0.005) with 25.7$\times$ NTP dominance (Table~\ref{tab:gradient-crossmodel}).

\begin{figure}[t]
  \centering
  \includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{fig_ss_multiseed.pdf}
  \caption{Multi-seed AR GAR under scheduled sampling ($\beta$\,=\,0 and $\beta$\,=\,5, three seeds). Steering nearly eliminates seed variance (std: 0.047$\to$0.001).}
  \label{fig:ss-multiseed}
\end{figure}

\subsection{Distribution Shift Analysis}\label{subsec:dist-shift}

The TF-AR GAR gap persists across all variants (Table~\ref{tab:four-way}). GAR-Dongre provides a control: it remains uniformly low (0.01--0.02) with no distribution gap, so the shift selectively destroys concentrated last-token attention while leaving diffuse attention unaffected. The mechanism is exposure bias \citep{bengio2015scheduled,zhang2019bridging,arora2022exposure}: during training the model conditions on ground-truth prefixes; at inference, on its own outputs. NTP self-recovers \citep{he2019exposure} because prediction is the same task at both regimes; GAR optimizes a distributional property with no autoregressive counterpart. Unlike token-level predictions, attention flow through layers \citep{abnar2020quantifying} over model-generated tokens has no training-time analogue, explaining why TF GAR-ours remains near $\tau$\,=\,0.5 while AR GAR collapses (77--87\% per-turn decay; Figure~\ref{fig:attention-heatmap}; scaling analysis in Appendix~\ref{sec:appendix-eb-scaling}; per-turn dynamics in Appendices~\ref{sec:appendix-ar-gar-dynamics} and~\ref{sec:appendix-perturn-detail}).

\begin{figure}[t]
  \centering
  \includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{fig3_gate_shift.pdf}
  \caption{(a)~Gate turn conditioning: per-layer gate values at turn~1 vs turn~21. In 27/28 layers, gates approach 1.0 at late turns, inverting the design intent ($p = 3.73 \times 10^{-8}$). (b)~Distribution shift ($N$\,=\,5 pilot): the TF-AR GAR gap persists across all five variants. Pilot gaps (20--34$\times$) exceed final $N$\,=\,50 estimates (8--17$\times$; Table~\ref{tab:four-way}) due to smaller sample size.}
  \label{fig:gate-shift}
\end{figure}

\subsection{Mechanism Specificity}\label{subsec:mechanism-specificity}

We test specificity with Turn-level Residual Coherence (TRC), the cosine similarity between adjacent-turn hidden states. TF/AR TRC is 0.334/0.332 for ThreadGate (gap $+$0.002) and 0.349/0.411 for LoRA-only (gap $-$0.062, reversed), confirming exposure bias disrupts a specific routing behavior while leaving representations intact. Cross-model evaluation confirms: GAR-Dongre shows negligible shift on both Llama (1.002$\times$) and Qwen (1.28$\times$) vs.\ the order-of-magnitude GAR-ours collapse.

\subsection{Cross-Model Replication}\label{subsec:cross-model}

We replicate on Llama-3.1-8B-Instruct \citep{grattafiori2024llama3} (Base, ThreadGate, LoRA-only; $N$\,=\,20, 4 turns). TF GAR-ours is 0.484 (TG) / 0.481 (LoRA-only); gating contributes zero benefit. The AR distribution is heavy-tailed (coefficient of variation, CoV\,$>$\,100\%); we report medians: 0.018 (TG and LoRA-only), 0.009 (Base), yielding gaps of 27$\times$ and 26$\times$ (Figure~\ref{fig:crossmodel}a in Appendix~\ref{sec:appendix-llama}). Gate profiles differ qualitatively (Figure~\ref{fig:crossmodel}b): Qwen attenuates early layers (L1\,=\,0.793) while Llama dips at late layers (L25\,=\,0.719), an architecture-dependent pattern, but convergence toward identity is universal (Appendix~\ref{sec:appendix-viz}). The gap is substantial on both architectures (8--17$\times$ on Qwen, 27$\times$ on Llama), consistent with exposure bias as architecture-independent.

\enlargethispage{2\baselineskip}
Scheduled sampling confirms cross-model generalization: Llama SS compresses the gap from ${\sim}$12$\times$ to 2.9$\times$ (TF GAR 0.477, AR GAR 0.166), weaker than Qwen's ${\sim}$1.3$\times$ likely due to fewer training epochs (2 vs.\ 3). The phase transition is architecture-dependent: $\beta$\,=\,3 triggers recovery on Qwen (0.556) but not Llama (0.173); Llama requires $\beta$\,=\,5 (GAR 0.791, exceeding Qwen SS 0.717), suggesting deeper architectures need higher steering thresholds but ultimately provide more surface for attention reweighting (full results in Appendix~\ref{sec:appendix-llama}). Cross-scale validation on Qwen-2.5-1.5B confirms the same pattern: 79.8\% GAR decay and $\beta$-steering recovery to 0.715 (Appendix~\ref{sec:appendix-1.5b}).

\section{Experiments}
\label{sec:experiments}

\subsection{Setup}

We fine-tune Qwen-2.5-7B-Instruct with LoRA \citep{hu2022lora,dettmers2023qlora} on 8K multi-turn conversations from the UltraChat \citep{ding2023ultrachat} training split. Training uses 3 epochs with AdamW (lr $= 2 \times 10^{-4}$, batch size 1, gradient accumulation 8, seed 42; ThreadGate additionally with seed 123); each variant requires approximately 6 hours on a single NVIDIA RTX PRO 6000 Blackwell Server Edition GPU (96\,GB; training costs in Appendix~\ref{sec:appendix-cost}). Full hyperparameters are in Appendix~\ref{sec:appendix-hyper}; dataset statistics in Appendix~\ref{sec:appendix-data}; the controlled comparison design is described in \S\ref{sec:comparison-design}. We evaluate under \emph{IID} (same-distribution UltraChat text) and \emph{held-out} (50 non-overlapping conversations) teacher-forced protocols, reporting GAR-ours, GAR-Dongre \citep{dongre2026attention}, and perplexity (PPL).

\subsection{Main Results}

Table~\ref{tab:four-way} presents the five-way comparison. Under TF evaluation, all fine-tuned variants converge: GAR-ours spans 0.49--0.51 ($\leq$2\,pp), PPL is 2.78 for all. IID and held-out protocols produce identical rankings (held-out GAR-ours differs by $\leq$1\,pp), ruling out data overlap as a confound. GAR-Dongre remains low (0.01--0.02) with no TF-AR gap, confirming that only concentrated last-token attention is vulnerable to distribution shift; scheduled sampling breaks this pattern (\S\ref{subsec:ss}).

Under AR generation ($N$\,=\,50), GAR-ours drops to 0.019--0.064, an 8--17$\times$ gap (Figure~\ref{fig:headline}). The ordering (ThreadGate 0.064 $>$ LoRA-only 0.037 $>$ Uniform Gate 0.033 $>$ PCGrad 0.030 $>$ Base 0.019) shows ThreadGate vs.\ LoRA-only is significant ($p$\,=\,0.004; Appendix~\ref{sec:appendix-stats}), yet the gap remains 7.8$\times$. A second seed confirms TF stability ($0.504\pm0.001$). MMLU 5-shot accuracy remains within 0.8\,pp across all variants (Appendix~\ref{sec:appendix-mmlu}). We analyze this distribution shift in \S\ref{sec:analysis}.

\subsection{Inference-Time Attention Steering}\label{subsec:steering}

\begin{figure}[t]
  \centering
  \includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{fig_ss_gap_compression.pdf}
  \caption{Scheduled sampling compresses the TF-AR gap from 7.8$\times$ to ${\sim}$1.3$\times$, the only variant approaching parity across five training conditions.}
  \label{fig:ss-gap}
\end{figure}

All training-time interventions leave the exposure bias gap intact. If the collapse is caused by attention misallocation rather than representation degradation, then directly correcting attention should recover GAR. We test this by adding a constant bias $\beta$ to pre-softmax attention logits for goal-token positions during generation:
\begin{equation}
  \tilde{A}_{i,j}^{(\ell,h)} = A_{i,j}^{(\ell,h)} + \beta \cdot \mathbf{1}[j \in \mathcal{G}],
\end{equation}
where $\tilde{A}$ denotes the modified logits before softmax. The additive form operates in log-probability space, providing uniform boost even when baseline attention is near zero. The response is sharply nonlinear: $\beta \in [0, 2]$ yields marginal improvement, then $\beta = 3$ triggers a phase transition to GAR\,=\,0.556 (9\% PPL cost). At $\beta$\,=\,5, GAR reaches 0.727$\pm$0.061 (PPL 1.77); the operating region $\beta \in [3, 5]$ exceeds the TF baseline while PPL remains below 1.8 (Figure~\ref{fig:steering-recovery}; full results in Appendix~\ref{sec:appendix-steering}; per-$\beta$ turn-level profiles in Appendix~\ref{sec:appendix-steering-viz}).

This closes the causal loop: if the collapse were due to representation drift in hidden states, no amount of attention reweighting could recover GAR. The recovery confirms attention misallocation, not representation degradation, as the proximal cause (qualitative examples in Appendix~\ref{sec:appendix-examples}).

\subsection{Scheduled Sampling Compresses the TF--AR Gap}\label{subsec:ss}

Scheduled sampling (SS; \citealp{bengio2015scheduled}), which mixes model-generated tokens into training prefixes with linearly increasing probability (implementation details in Appendix~\ref{sec:appendix-ss}), directly targets the training-inference mismatch identified in \S\ref{subsec:dist-shift}. Across three seeds, AR GAR-ours rises from 0.064 to $0.401\pm0.047$ (seeds: 0.347 / 0.423 / 0.433; per-seed details in Appendix~\ref{sec:appendix-perseed}), compressing the gap from 7.8$\times$ to ${\sim}$1.3$\times$ (per-seed gaps: 1.45$\times$, 1.17$\times$, 1.15$\times$; TF GAR $0.498\pm0.004$) with no PPL cost ($1.441\pm0.001$; Figure~\ref{fig:ss-gap}). Steering on SS models is remarkably stable ($\beta$\,=\,5: $0.717\pm0.001$): the training-time seed variance (std\,=\,0.047 at $\beta$\,=\,0) is nearly eliminated (Figure~\ref{fig:ss-multiseed}), suggesting $\beta$-steering converges to a seed-independent attention equilibrium. The improvement generalizes across GAR definitions: SS raises mid-turn GAR above 0.36 under all four alternative token-position variants (Appendix~\ref{sec:appendix-gar-robust}), while ThreadGate only improves last-token GAR (0.325 vs.\ all-token 0.025): SS builds a broad goal-attention pattern, not position-specific overfitting. GAR-Dongre also rises substantially under SS ($N$\,=\,5 pilot; see Appendix~\ref{sec:appendix-n5}), the only intervention that moves both metrics. The benefit generalizes across architectures: on Llama-3.1-8B, SS compresses the gap from ${\sim}$12$\times$ to 2.9$\times$ (AR GAR 0.166, TF GAR 0.477), weaker than Qwen's ${\sim}$1.3$\times$ likely due to fewer training epochs (2 vs.\ 3; \S\ref{subsec:cross-model}). The benefit diminishes in longer dialogues (Appendix~\ref{sec:appendix-n5}), consistent with exposure bias compounding over turns.
